 "label": "Getting sick",
 "topic": "Body & health",
 "rationale": "Coming down with something: catching a cold, catching one from someone, a real illness, getting run down and an upset stomach.",
 "mixUps": "kaze o hiku is getting a cold; kaze ga utsuru is catching it from someone. byouki ni naru sounds like a real illness, so for a rough few days say taichou kuzushita. kuzusu and kowasu both mean break: taichou o kuzusu for your health in general, onaka o kowasu for your stomach. kaze 'cold' sounds like kaze 'wind'.",
 "targets": [
  {
   "id": "c-kaze-o-hiku",
   "romaji": "kaze o hiku",
   "polite": "kaze o hikimasu",
   "english": "catch a cold",
   "ja": "風邪を引く",
   "kana": "かぜをひく",
   "politeJa": "風邪を引きます",
   "noun": "kaze",
   "particle": "o",
   "verb": "hiku",
   "verbJa": "引く",
   "verbSense": "pull, draw; with a cold it means come down with",
   "englishVerb": "catch",
   "trap": "Not kaze o toru or tsukamu (catch a ball): you 'pull' a cold, kaze o hiku (kaze hiichatta).",
   "partners": [],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Kaze hiita (or hiichatta) = I've caught a cold; kaze hiiteru = I have a cold right now, not 'I'm catching one'. Usually said without o.",
   "notes": "Kaze 'cold' sounds exactly like kaze 'wind' (kaze ga fuku = the wind blows), so this phrase is how 'a cold' is learned. Flu doesn't take hiku: infuru ni kakatta, infuru ni natta. Getting over a cold is kaze ga naoru (kaze, mou naotta?).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "kaze hiichatta mitai.",
    "ja": "風邪ひいちゃったみたい。",
    "english": "I think I've caught a cold."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "kaze o hiita mitai desu.",
    "ja": "風邪をひいたみたいです。",
    "english": "I think I've caught a cold."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-kaze-ga-utsuru",
   "romaji": "kaze ga utsuru",
   "polite": "kaze ga utsurimasu",
   "english": "catch someone's cold",
   "ja": "風邪がうつる",
   "kana": "かぜがうつる",
   "politeJa": "風邪がうつります",
   "noun": "kaze",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "utsuru",
   "verbJa": "うつる",
   "verbSense": "move, spread (to another place or person)",
   "englishVerb": "catch",
   "trap": "Kaze o hiku is just getting a cold; to say you caught it from someone, the cold moves to you, kaze ga utsuru.",
   "partners": [
    "infuru (flu)",
    "akubi (a yawn)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Heard as utsuru yo (you'll catch it) and kaze utsutta or utsutchatta (I caught it off someone). Say who from with kara (kodomo kara utsutta) or no (kodomo no kaze ga utsutta). Just as common: kaze o morau, 'receive' the cold (kodomo kara kaze moratchatta).",
   "notes": "Giving your cold to someone else is kaze o utsusu: kaze utsushichatta, gomen (sorry, I gave you my cold).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "kodomo no kaze, utsutchatta.",
    "ja": "子供の風邪、うつっちゃった。",
    "english": "I caught my kid's cold."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "kodomo no kaze ga utsutta mitai desu.",
    "ja": "子供の風邪がうつったみたいです。",
    "english": "I think I caught my child's cold."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-byouki-ni-naru",
   "romaji": "byouki ni naru",
   "polite": "byouki ni narimasu",
   "english": "get sick, fall ill",
   "ja": "病気になる",
   "kana": "びょうきになる",
   "politeJa": "病気になります",
   "noun": "byouki",
   "particle": "ni",
   "verb": "naru",
   "verbJa": "なる",
   "verbSense": "become, turn into",
   "englishVerb": "get",
   "trap": "English 'I got sick' is usually something minor, but byouki ni natta sounds like a real illness: for a cold or a rough few days say kaze hiita, guai ga waruku natta or taichou kuzushita. Byouki desu ('I'm sick') sounds more serious still.",
   "partners": [
